\title{The Cyclic Splitting-Type Channel:\\ Exact Laws, Divisor Bounds, and the Breaking of the One-Bit Pair Cap}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $f$ be a prime and let $\Q(\zeta_f)$ be the corresponding cyclotomic field, with cyclic Galois group $C_n$, $n=f-1$. To each unramified rational prime $p$ we attach its \emph{splitting type} $T(p)=\ord_f(p)$, the residue degree of $p$ in $\Q(\zeta_f)$; this is the complete invariant of the decomposition of $p$, and an exact function of $p \bmod f$. Modelling Frobenius equidistribution by a uniform variable $x$ on $C_n$ with $T(x)=n/\gcd(n,x)$, we study the single-prime type channel, with entropy $\HT(n)$, and the semiprime type-pair channel $\Ipair(n)=H(\Pi)-\frac1n\sum_c H(\Pi_c)$, the mutual information between the product class $N\equiv x+y$ and the unordered type pair $\{T(x),T(y)\}$. We prove an exact closed form for $\Ipair(p)$ at every prime cyclic order, sharp two-sided quadratic asymptotics, and the sub-cap theorem: $\Ipair(p)<1$ for every odd prime, with $p=2$ the unique prime attaining the one-bit binary-fork cap, while exhaustive evaluation gives $\Ipair(n)>1$ for $n=4,6,8,10,12,14,16$. We prove the Chinese-Remainder factorisation $T_{mn}=T_mT_n=\lcmop(T_m,T_n)$ for coprime moduli and the resulting exact additivity $\HT(mn)=\HT(m)+\HT(n)$, hence a Sylow decomposition; the closed form $\HT(2^k)=2-2^{1-k}$, so the $2$-adic tower saturates at exactly two bits; the divisor-lattice sandwich $\Hnr(n)\le\HT(n)\le\log_2 d(n)$, strict for $n\ge3$; and the dichotomy that the binary root-count readout is lossy precisely at composite orders, with the loss tending to the full two bits along the $2$-tower. Finally we show that coprime additivity of the pair channel is incompatible with the naive assertion that all odd orders stay below the cap, and give the explicit witness.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} cyclotomic field, Frobenius splitting type, Chebotarev equidistribution, mutual information, Euler totient law, divisor lattice, semiprime channel.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The one-bit phenomenon}

A recurring question in the information-theoretic study of arithmetic is: how much does a product of two primes reveal about its factors, short of factoring? The standard setting attaches to each prime $p$ a binary arithmetic label $\varepsilon(p)\in\{\pm1\}$ --- a Legendre symbol, a split/inert indicator, a residue bit --- and asks for the mutual information between $N=pq$ (equivalently, its residue class) and the \emph{unordered} pair $\{\varepsilon(p),\varepsilon(q)\}$.

For every symmetric binary label of this kind the answer is at most one bit, and the bound is attained: knowing $N \bmod f$ determines the \emph{product} $\varepsilon(p)\varepsilon(q)$ exactly and nothing more. We call this the \emph{binary-fork cap}. It is often read as a structural obstruction. The results below show that it is an artifact of the \emph{instrument}: the binary label is a lossy coarsening of a genuinely multi-state observable, and the complete observable breaks the cap.

\subsection{The complete observable}

For $\Q(\zeta_f)$ with $f$ prime, the Galois group is $(\Z/f)^\times\cong C_n$ with $n=f-1$, and the Frobenius class of an unramified prime $p$ is $p \bmod f$. The \emph{splitting type} $T(p)=\ord_f(p)$ is the residue degree: $p$ factors into exactly $n/T(p)$ distinct primes of $\Q(\zeta_f)$, each of residue degree $T(p)$. This is a complete description of the splitting behaviour and a deterministic function of $p \bmod f$, so the mutual information between the residue class and the type is exactly $H(T)$; refining to $p \bmod f^k$ adds nothing.

The traditional binary readout is the coarsening $\nr(p)=\mathbf 1[T(p)=1]$, ``does $p$ split completely?''. For $f=5$ this collapses $T=2$ (two primes of degree two) and $T=4$ (inert) into a single symbol. Quantifying that collapse, and its consequence for the semiprime channel, is the content of this paper.

\section{The probabilistic model}

By the Chebotarev density theorem the Frobenius classes of the unramified primes are equidistributed: for $\Q(\zeta_f)$ each class in $(\Z/f)^\times$ has natural density $1/n$. All statements below are therefore statements about the following finite model, and translate to densities of primes verbatim.

\begin{definition}[Splitting type]
Let $n\ge1$ and let $x$ be uniform on $C_n=\Z/n$ (written additively). Set
\[
T_n(x)=\frac{n}{\gcd(n,x)},
\]
the order of $x$ in $C_n$; under $C_n\cong\operatorname{Gal}(\Q(\zeta_f)/\Q)$ this is the residue degree $\ord_f(p)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Totient law]\label{prop:totient}
$\Prob(T_n=d)=\varphi(d)/n$ for every divisor $d\mid n$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The elements of order $d$ in a cyclic group of order $n$ are the $\varphi(d)$ generators of its unique subgroup of order $d$.
\end{proof}

Consequently, with $H$ the Shannon entropy in bits,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:HT}
\HT(n)=-\sum_{d\mid n}\frac{\varphi(d)}{n}\log_2\frac{\varphi(d)}{n}
=\log_2 n-\frac1n\sum_{d\mid n}\varphi(d)\log_2\varphi(d).
\end{equation}

\begin{example}
$\HT(4)=2-\tfrac14(2\cdot1)=1.5$ exactly, realised in $\Q(\zeta_5)$ by the types $\{1,2,4\}$ with densities $\tfrac14,\tfrac14,\tfrac12$; $\HT(6)=\log_26-\tfrac46=1.9183\ldots$, realised in $\Q(\zeta_7)$ by $\{1,2,3,6\}$ with densities $\tfrac16,\tfrac16,\tfrac13,\tfrac13$. Sampling actual primes reproduces $1.4989$ and $1.9183$.
\end{example}

\begin{definition}[Root-count readout]
$\nr_n(x)=\mathbf 1[T_n(x)=1]$, with occupation numbers $[1,n-1]$, so that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Hnr}
\Hnr(n)=h_2\!\left(\tfrac1n\right)=\log_2 n-\frac{n-1}{n}\log_2(n-1).
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Semiprime type-pair channel]
Let $x,y$ be independent uniform on $C_n$, modelling the Frobenius classes of two independent primes, so that $N=pq$ has class $c=x+y$. Let $K(x,y)=\{T_n(x),T_n(y)\}$ be the unordered type pair, $\Pi$ its law over all $n^2$ ordered pairs, and $\Pi_c$ its law conditioned on $x+y=c$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Ipair}
\Ipair(n)=H(\Pi)-\frac1n\sum_{c\in C_n}H(\Pi_c).
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

Three remarks fix the interpretation. The channel is symmetric under swapping the two primes; the ``which factor is which'' information measured on genuine semiprimes is $0.0001$ bits, i.e.\ numerically zero. Everything is finite and exact: $\Pi$ and each $\Pi_c$ are integer occupation tables of masses $n^2$ and $n$, so \eqref{eq:Ipair} is an explicit finite sum of logarithms of rationals. Finally, replacing $T$ by $\nr$ in \eqref{eq:Ipair} yields the classical split-count channel, a strictly smaller \emph{face} of the type channel.

